{"subjectId":"sql","id":"median-running-difference","title":"Median, Percentiles & Running Differences","group":"Interview Prep","order":61,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">There is no universal <code>MEDIAN()</code> in SQL. Interviewers ask you to build it from window functions — and to compute running totals and row-to-row differences.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-math-function\"></i>Median with ROW_NUMBER and COUNT</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- works for odd and even counts: average the middle one or two rows\nWITH ordered AS (\n    SELECT salary,\n           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY salary) AS rn,\n           COUNT(*)     OVER ()                AS n\n    FROM employees\n)\nSELECT AVG(salary) AS median_salary\nFROM ordered\nWHERE rn IN ((n + 1) / 2, (n + 2) / 2);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>median_salary\n-------------\n90000.0</code></pre></figure>\n<p>With 10 salaries, integer division gives rows 5 and 6 (85000 and 95000) → 90000. With 9 rows both expressions give 5, the single middle row.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- median per department\nWITH ordered AS (\n    SELECT dept_id, salary,\n           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id ORDER BY salary) AS rn,\n           COUNT(*)     OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id)                AS n\n    FROM employees\n    WHERE dept_id IS NOT NULL\n)\nSELECT dept_id, AVG(salary) AS median_salary\nFROM ordered\nWHERE rn IN ((n + 1) / 2, (n + 2) / 2)\nGROUP BY dept_id\nORDER BY dept_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>dept_id | median_salary\n--------+--------------\n1       | 107500.0\n2       | 72000.0\n3       | 82500.0</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQLite (recent)</figcaption><pre><code>-- recent SQLite builds (3.53 here) include median() and percentile()\nSELECT median(salary) AS median, ROUND(percentile(salary, 90)) AS p90 FROM employees;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>median  | p90\n--------+---------\n90000.0 | 123000.0</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>PostgreSQL / Oracle / SQL Server / Snowflake</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY salary) AS median,     -- interpolated\n       PERCENTILE_DISC(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY salary) AS median_disc -- an actual value\nFROM employees;                                         -- PostgreSQL / Oracle (SQL Server: OVER ())\nSELECT MEDIAN(salary) FROM employees;                   -- Oracle, Snowflake</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-trending-up\"></i>Running total and running difference</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>WITH monthly AS (\n    SELECT strftime('%Y-%m', o.order_date) AS month, SUM(oi.quantity * oi.unit_price) AS revenue\n    FROM orders o JOIN order_items oi ON oi.order_id = o.order_id\n    WHERE o.status &lt;&gt; 'cancelled'\n    GROUP BY month\n)\nSELECT month, revenue,\n       SUM(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month ROWS UNBOUNDED PRECEDING) AS running_total,\n       revenue - LAG(revenue, 1, 0) OVER (ORDER BY month)            AS diff_from_prev\nFROM monthly\nORDER BY month;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>month   | revenue | running_total | diff_from_prev\n--------+---------+---------------+---------------\n2024-01 | 66700   | 66700         | 66700\n2024-02 | 12900   | 79600         | -53800\n2024-03 | 63700   | 143300        | 50800</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-diff\"></i>Difference from the group's first / previous value</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT name, hire_date, salary,\n       salary - FIRST_VALUE(salary) OVER (ORDER BY hire_date) AS vs_first_hire,\n       julianday(hire_date) - julianday(LAG(hire_date) OVER (ORDER BY hire_date)) AS days_since_prev_hire\nFROM employees\nWHERE dept_id = 1\nORDER BY hire_date;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name  | hire_date  | salary | vs_first_hire | days_since_prev_hire\n------+------------+--------+---------------+---------------------\nAsha  | 2015-03-01 | 150000 | 0             | NULL\nRavi  | 2017-06-15 | 120000 | -30000        | 837.0\nMeera | 2019-01-10 | 95000  | -55000        | 574.0\nKaran | 2020-08-01 | 95000  | -55000        | 569.0</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you calculate a median in SQL?</summary><div><p>Portable: number rows with <code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY x)</code> and count them with <code>COUNT(*) OVER ()</code>, then average the rows at positions <code>(n+1)/2</code> and <code>(n+2)/2</code> (integer division). Engine functions: <code>PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY x)</code> (PostgreSQL, Oracle, SQL Server), <code>MEDIAN(x)</code> (Oracle, Snowflake, recent SQLite builds).</p></div></details></section>"}